domain ADR0030Acceptance:
  description "Record the product owner acceptance of exact ADR-0030 revision 1."

problem P1:
  |
    ADR-0030 revision 1 was Proposed and implementation remained blocked until
    exact owner acceptance was recorded in the authoritative think file.

evidence E_TARGET:
  |
    The reviewed target was docs/adr/0030-llm-assisted-character-semantic-migration.think
    revision 1, paired with its Markdown projection and complete Japanese review
    translation. The Proposed ADR Seal before acceptance was 417496dc4365… and
    the Japanese review-input Seal was b1cfdf5e9890….

evidence E_REVIEW:
  |
    The owner was presented the purpose, material differences, decision points,
    alternatives, risks, unknowns, and full Japanese review scope for exact
    ADR-0030 revision 1.

evidence OWNER_ACCEPTANCE:
  |
    On 2026-09-10 the product owner replied ACCEPT to the explicit request to
    decide ADR-0030 revision 1 as ACCEPT, REVISE, or REJECT.

premise SCOPE:
  |
    Acceptance authorizes the ADR architectural direction and its local successor
    implementation gates. It does not itself authorize provider calls, deployment,
    production reads or writes, candidate acceptance, pointer movement, rollback,
    or authoring-policy activation; those remain separate gates in the ADR and plan.

premise OPTIONS:
  |
    A leave the ADR Proposed despite exact acceptance. B record OWNER_ACCEPTANCE
    and ACCEPTANCE in the authoritative think, project Accepted status to Markdown
    and the ADR index, finish PERT task cb202, and Seal the accepted revision and
    affected dependants.

decision D1 based_on P1, E_TARGET, E_REVIEW, OWNER_ACCEPTANCE, SCOPE, OPTIONS:
  |
    Choose B. Preserve every substantive decision of revision 1, add only
    acceptance evidence and lifecycle decision, update the projection and index,
    close cb202, audit all changed ADR reasoning, inspect Seal impact, and reseal
    affected records. Do not begin cb203 or any external action in this acceptance
    recording step.
